{"id":"1185443e236845a6b6f02a64956118de","model":"gpt-oss-120b","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"answer\": \"noanswer\",\n  \"citations\": []\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to answer: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" Need to interpret: Which UK Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party? Possibly referring to a Prime Minister who was succeeded (i.e., replaced) by the Leader of the Conservative Party in the 1990s. In 1990s, Conservative Party leaders: John Major became leader in 1990 after Margaret Thatcher resigned as PM. So Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major (who was Leader of the Conservative Party). The question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" That seems to ask: Which PM was succeeded by the Conservative Party leader? In 1990s, Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major (who was Leader of the Conservative Party). So answer: Margaret Thatcher.\n\nWe need evidence. Look at evidence: In United Kingdom local elections 1980 text: \"These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.\" That shows Margaret Thatcher was Conservative PM. Need evidence that John Major succeeded her in 1990s. Not directly in given evidence. We have evidence about 1997 Conservative Party leadership election triggered when John Major resigned after 1997 election. That shows John Major was leader and PM before 1997. But need evidence that Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major in 1990s. Not present. Could we infer from \"The 1997 Conservative Party leadership election was triggered ... when John Major resigned ... following his party's defeat at the 1997 general election, which ended 18 years of Conservative Government\". That indicates John Major was PM before 1997, but not who he succeeded. No direct evidence.\n\nWe have evidence about \"The Federation of Conservative Students ... broken up by Chairman Norman Tebbit after ...\". Not relevant.\n\nWe have evidence about \"Benjamin Disraeli ...\". Not relevant.\n\nThus we lack direct citation that Margaret Thatcher was succeeded by John Major. However we have evidence that Margaret Thatcher was PM (from local elections 1980). And we have evidence that John Major was leader and PM (from 1997 leadership election). But need citation that she was succeeded by him. Not present. Could we use \"The 1997 Conservative Party leadership election was triggered ... when John Major resigned ... following his party's defeat ... which ended 18 years of Conservative Government\". That implies Conservative government started 1979 (Thatcher) and lasted 18 years until 1997, so Thatcher preceded Major. But not explicit.\n\nThe question: \"What United Kingdom Prime Minister was succeeded in the 1990s by the Leader of the Conservative Party?\" Could also be referring to \"James Callaghan was succeeded by Michael Foot\" but that's Labour. Not.\n\nMaybe they ask: Which PM was succeeded by the Leader of the Conservative Party (i.e., the Conservative Party leader succeeded the PM). In 1990s, John Major succeeded Margaret Thatcher as PM, and he was also Leader of the Conservative Party. So answer: Margaret Thatcher.\n\nWe need citation for Margaret Thatcher being PM (we have). Also need citation for John Major succeeding her? Not present. Could we cite that John Major was Conservative Party leader after Thatcher? Not present. The evidence about 1997 leadership election says John Major resigned as leader after defeat, but doesn't mention Thatcher. However we could use that John Major was leader and PM, and that Thatcher was PM earlier, but not explicit succession.\n\nGiven requirement: answer must be supported by citations. If we cannot find direct citation linking succession, maybe answer \"noanswer\". But we can maybe argue that the question asks which PM was succeeded by the Leader of the Conservative Party in the 1990s. The evidence shows Margaret Thatcher was PM (1980 local elections). Also we have evidence that John Major was Conservative Party leader (1997 leadership election). However not explicit that he succeeded her. Might be considered enough? Might be borderline.\n\nBetter to answer \"noanswer\" due to lack of direct evidence. But we have partial. The policy: must cite evidence for answer. If answer not directly supported, we should answer noanswer.\n\nThus answer noanswer with empty citations? Must provide citations list (maybe empty). According to spec, citations list of pairs. If no answer, maybe empty list. Provide answer \"noanswer\"."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1734,"completion_tokens":931,"total_tokens":2665,"audio_prompt_tokens":0},"created":1791982381,"object":"chat.completion","prompt_filter_results":null}